4. Question Two – Batch Processing

Batch processing can help automate multiple tasks on a computer. ApliSoft can use a batch script to copy over important files to a back-up folder.

4.1 Example Batch Script

@echo off
title ApliSoft Automatic Backup
set "SOURCE=%USERPROFILE%\Documents\ApliSoftFiles"
set "DEST=%USERPROFILE%\Desktop\ApliSoftBackup"
echo Starting ApliSoft Backup...
if not exist "%SOURCE%" (
echo The source folder does not exist.
pause
exit /b 1
)
if not exist "%DEST%" mkdir "%DEST%"
robocopy "%SOURCE%" "%DEST%" /E /R:1 /W:1
set "RESULT=%ERRORLEVEL%"
if %RESULT% GEQ 8 (
echo Backup failed please check the folders.
pause
exit /b %RESULT%
)
echo Backup Complete.
echo Robocopy exited with code %RESULT%.
pause

Figure 2.1 – ApliSoft Backup Batch Script Code

Figure 2.2 – Successful Backup Script Execution

4.2 Explanation & Benefits

The SOURCE and DEST variables are set to help with the script.

It first checks if the source folder exists, if it doesn’t it will show an error message and end the script. If the destination folder doesn’t exist it will create the folder.

The robocopy command will copy over the files. The /E flag will copy over sub-folders. The /R:1 flag will allow one retry and /W:1 will wait one second between retries.

Generally exit codes of less than 8 will show there was no fatal error during the copy. Exit codes of 8 or higher will show there was an error [4].

This helps save time and reduce repetition but is a simple method of copying files. There is no versioning and the business should have separate back-ups.

